WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.870
<v Jamie>Meta's down nine point six-five percent, Microsoft up fifteen point three-three—the AI trade just split the market in half, and I'm watching one side get left at the station.

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:34.877
<v Alex>Meta reported earnings after hours yesterday, and the selloff didn't pause—it accelerated. Down another eight percent in extended trading, which means the market's not even waiting for the morning bell to decide this is worse than expected. Gross margin guidance came in soft, and now traders are repricing the entire AI capex story: maybe throwing one hundred and sixty billion dollars at infrastructure doesn't guarantee returns if your ad product can't actually monetize the improvement.

00:00:34.877 --> 00:01:03.503
<v Alex>S&P up one point three-four percent, Nasdaq up two point six-one—and that's all on the back of a clean AI bifurcation. Microsoft is up fifteen point three-three percent, Broadcom up four point six, Oracle up eight point two-nine. Meanwhile Meta's still bleeding—down nine point six-five at the open, and the V I X just cratered ten point seven percent, which tells you the market's not panicking, it's just reallocating. Risk-on day, but only if your company has the word 'infrastructure' or 'chip' in the pitch deck.

00:01:03.503 --> 00:01:09.425
<v Jamie>So Microsoft gets to be the hero because it's not Meta. That's the entire trade right now?

00:01:09.425 --> 00:01:37.064
<v Maya>It's worse than that—Microsoft's margin profile on AI is completely different. They're selling the shovels and the gold, Meta's trying to be the gold. Adobe down six point four-nine, Accenture down five point eight-three, Salesforce down five point three-seven—every company that said 'our AI will make you money' is getting repriced because Meta just showed the receipt and it doesn't add up. The market's not just punishing Meta, it's questioning whether the entire 'throw billions at AI and monetize later' thesis actually works.

00:01:37.064 --> 00:01:41.999
<v Jamie>Which is a question Mark Zuckerberg did not want asked in front of everyone.
  ↳ source: https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/29/mark-zuckerberg-predicts-that-billions-of-people-will-have-personal-ai-agents-in-five-years/

00:01:41.999 --> 00:01:54.831
<v Alex>The dollar's down, which means traders are betting the Fed doesn't hike again, but they're also hedging—buying gold because they're not entirely sure what happens when the AI capex bill comes due and nobody's revenue actually covers it.

00:01:54.831 --> 00:02:14.244
<v Jamie>Meta's free cash flow just cratered, and that's the real wound here. We're talking about a company that spent one hundred and sixty billion dollars on AI infrastructure, and the return signal just went dark. Revenue guidance missed, margins compressed—and Zuckerberg's response was essentially 'trust the process.' The market heard 'we have no idea when this pays off.'
  ↳ source: https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/29/mark-zuckerberg-predicts-that-billions-of-people-will-have-personal-ai-agents-in-five-years/

00:02:14.244 --> 00:02:31.354
<v Alex>The gross margin miss is what's actually disqualifying. Meta's AI capex story only works if you can show improving unit economics on the backend—better ad targeting, higher CPMs, something. Instead, they guided lower and the market's now doing the math: maybe the infrastructure spend is a cost center, not an investment.

00:02:31.354 --> 00:02:58.005
<v Maya>And here's the thing—Microsoft's doing the exact same thing with Azure capex, but they're selling it as a service, so they get paid first. Meta's betting the house on their own ad product improving. That's not a capex story, that's a product bet, and product bets fail all the time. Microsoft's basically saying 'pay us to build your AI,' Meta's saying 'wait while we build ours and maybe ads get better.' Investors are choosing the one that gets cash today.

00:02:58.005 --> 00:03:15.115
<v Jamie>So the FTC lawsuit against Meta just became a weight around the neck of an already sinking ship. You've got regulatory pressure, a missed quarter, and now the enterprise AI pitch that was supposed to justify all this spending—the thing Zuckerberg kept calling 'the future of AI'—just landed with a thud.

00:03:15.115 --> 00:03:31.896
<v Alex>Peers are getting shredded too. Adobe down six point four-nine, Accenture down five point eight-three. Every company that built their guidance on 'AI will unlock new revenue streams' is now being asked to show the math. Meta was supposed to be the proof of concept. Instead, they're the cautionary tale.

00:03:31.896 --> 00:03:51.309
<v Maya>The really brutal part? Zuckerberg's been running 'the year of efficiency' for two years, cutting costs, talking about doing more with less—and the moment he shows the market what 'less' actually looks like in terms of returns, investors panic. You can't talk about being lean and then ask for patience on a one-hundred-sixty-billion-dollar bet at the same time.
  ↳ source: https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/29/mark-zuckerberg-predicts-that-billions-of-people-will-have-personal-ai-agents-in-five-years/

00:03:51.309 --> 00:04:04.141
<v Jamie>Microsoft gets the halo. Meta gets the reset. And everyone else in the middle—everyone who said 'AI will save our margins'—is now being valued like their AI strategy might actually not work. That's the bifurcation. That's what today is.

00:04:04.141 --> 00:04:14.670
<v Maya>FTC just sued Hims & Hers for selling patient medical data to Meta and Snap—we're talking sexual wellness and mental health records, tracked through website pixels and handed straight to advertisers.

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:23.883
<v Jamie>Wait, so the entire business model was 'you come to us with erectile dysfunction, we sell that fact to Meta, and then Meta sells you a boat'?

00:04:23.883 --> 00:04:33.425
<v Maya>That's the allegation. Hims built the telehealth unicorn story on convenience and privacy, then monetized the data stream instead of the prescriptions. They just inverted the whole thing.

00:04:33.425 --> 00:04:45.599
<v Alex>Privacy policy as a lie detector test. Investors see 'H I P A A-compliant' in the deck, regulators see 'we're selling everything to the highest bidder.' The gap between those two readings is now a lawsuit.

00:04:45.599 --> 00:04:50.534
<v Maya>Zoox—Amazon's robotaxi—just cleared the final federal safety exemption. They're legal to charge passengers now.

00:04:50.534 --> 00:04:59.747
<v Jamie>They got the stamp. Now they have to prove they can actually operate a car service without the car deciding a fire hydrant is a speed bump.

00:04:59.747 --> 00:05:09.947
<v Alex>The exemption is temporary. They've got regulatory permission to run rides, but the real test is whether a custom-built robotaxi can generate revenue faster than it racks up insurance claims.

00:05:09.947 --> 00:05:16.528
<v Jamie>Ferrari raised guidance after beating Q2 earnings. Luxury demand is still intact while the rest of the market's recalibrating.

00:05:16.528 --> 00:05:27.057
<v Maya>Of course the car that costs hundreds of thousands of dollars is holding up fine. Billionaires aren't buying Ferraris because of AI margin expansion—they're buying them because they're bored and rich.

00:05:27.057 --> 00:05:41.863
<v Alex>Luxury goods don't care about the capex thesis. They care about wealth concentration and ego. Meta's trying to prove a return on one hundred and sixty billion in infrastructure spend. Ferrari's just making sure the person who buys the car looks cool doing it.

00:05:41.863 --> 00:05:48.773
<v Maya>Robinhood's crypto trading revenue dropped thirty-eight percent—the retail enthusiasm that carried the whole sector through recent years is just gone.
  ↳ source: https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/crypto/articles/robinhood-crypto-trading-revenue-plunges-132800220.html

00:05:48.773 --> 00:05:57.986
<v Jamie>The app that convinced millions of people to trade options like lottery tickets is now watching those same people realize crypto isn't actually a wealth transfer machine.

00:05:57.986 --> 00:06:12.134
<v Maya>Health authorities just issued a warning about cyclospora—that's a parasite that causes what they're calling 'explosive diarrhoea'—and they're tracking confirmed cases, mostly travellers coming back from Mexico. The parasite has better branding than most crypto projects and a more reliable user base.

00:06:12.134 --> 00:06:29.244
<v Jamie>So you go to Cancun for a week, come home with a souvenir that actively ruins your digestive system, and health authorities have to send out a public health advisory because it's spreading. That's the downside of the travel boom nobody talks about—not just jet lag, actual biological warfare from salad.

00:06:29.244 --> 00:06:45.695
<v Alex>That's not an outbreak, that's a pattern. And the fact that they're all linked to Mexico travel means someone's going to start pricing this into travel insurance, which means the cost of a beach vacation just went up by whatever the underwriting team decides 'parasitic infection risk' is worth.

00:06:45.695 --> 00:07:02.147
<v Jamie>On the tape tomorrow: we've got a cluster of earnings landing July thirtieth—Acadian Asset Management, A D T, American Electric Power, A G C O, Agios Pharma, Allegro Microsystems all reporting. That's six different sectors hitting at once, which means the market's going to have to pick a lane.

00:07:02.147 --> 00:07:11.360
<v Alex>Utilities like American Electric Power matter right now because the rate environment just shifted. If A E P's guidance assumes lower rates, the stock gets repriced immediately.

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:24.850
<v Maya>Agios Pharmaceuticals is the one I'm watching because rare disease biotech doesn't get repriced on macro—it moves on pipeline data. If they've got clinical updates or revenue surprises, it'll actually move independent of whatever the Fed's doing with the dollar.

00:07:24.850 --> 00:07:40.972
<v Jamie>So we get a utilities stress test, a biotech wildcard, and five other earnings that'll either confirm the bifurcation we saw today or prove the market's starting to rotate out of it. Wednesday's going to tell us whether this is a one-day repricing or the beginning of something longer.

00:07:40.972 --> 00:07:49.198
<v Alex>Meta spent one hundred and sixty billion dollars to prove that scale doesn't buy you a business model. They're still looking for the receipt.

00:07:49.198 --> 00:07:53.147
<v Maya>The receipt's in the mail. Probably stuck behind the cyclospora warning.

00:07:53.147 --> 00:07:57.753
<v Jamie>This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
